.TH OOBE-GXWI 1 2026-10-19 "Peios installer 0.1.13"
.SH NAME
oobe-gxwi \- Peios first-boot setup, in a browser, as GXWI's overlay
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B oobe-gxwi
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR ADDRESS : PORT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B oobe-gxwi
presents the Multi-Surface Interaction Protocol (MSIP) conversation owned by
.BR oobed (8)
as a GXWI browser overlay.
The daemon performs the privileged operations.
All connected browsers observe and modify the same conversation.
Closing a browser does not cancel a daemon job.
.PP
GXWI starts the frontend with an inherited Unix listening socket and handles
the external browser connection.
The frontend serves HTTP/2 over that socket.
For development,
.B \-\-listen
starts a direct HTTP listener without GXWI's TLS termination.
All browser assets are embedded in the executable.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
.BR oobed 's
socket.
The default is
.IR /run/oobed.sock .
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " ADDRESS" : PORT
Serve here instead of on a socket handed over, for working on the page where
there is no GXWI.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of these.
.SH SECURITY
The overlay is available without normal sign-in.
Coordinate with other people using it and keep the machine on a trusted
network until setup completes.
Verify the GXWI certificate through the machine's trusted console before
entering a password.
.PP
Direct development listeners serve unencrypted HTTP.
Use synthetic credentials and an isolated environment for those listeners.
The frontend relies on GXWI for external connection security and on the
daemon's socket access controls for its MSIP connection.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Help, version, or normal server completion.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments, missing inherited socket, or server initialization error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LISTEN_FDS
Must indicate at least one inherited descriptor when
.B \-\-listen
is absent.
The frontend takes ownership of descriptor 3 as its Unix listener.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/oobed.sock
Default conversation socket.
.SH LIMITS
Incoming WebSocket messages and frames are limited to 1 MiB.
The supplied browser page refuses oversized submissions with a visible
message; the server also enforces the limit.
A request within that limit can still be refused if its encoded MSIP answer
exceeds the daemon protocol limit.
The page reports that refusal rather than replaying the request.
.PP
The shared browser-input queue admits at most 4 MiB of accounted data.
Accounting includes the raw message and fixed entry overhead, and remains
reserved until handling finishes.
When the queue is full, a browser connection waits for capacity while it
continues receiving view updates.
Each connection can hold one additional capped frame outside that queue,
waiting for admission or for its previous action to finish.
.PP
This is not a bound on total process memory.
The number of active browser connections is not globally capped.
Browser view updates and close frames have a 10-second write deadline.
A timeout ends that browser connection.
.PP
If writes to the daemon make no progress for 10 seconds, the frontend
abandons that connection and presents the lost-connection state while it
tries to reconnect.
.SH NOTES
Browser input is associated with the daemon connection that received it.
When that connection changes, the frontend closes the old browser socket
with WebSocket code 1012 so the page can reconnect to fresh state.
Queued actions belonging to the old connection are discarded rather than
replayed against another conversation.
.PP
An action already sent to the daemon can have taken effect before the
connection was lost.
Inspect the recovered state before repeating an action.
A reconnect does not roll back a running installation or setup operation.
Closing a tab does not guarantee that an earlier submission was canceled.
Connection cleanup follows an observed close or error; a close message can
arrive behind previously sent data.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gxwid (1),
.BR installer-gxwi (1),
.BR oobed (8)
